WEBVTT

00:00:00.071 --> 00:00:01.788
If your treatment card says STRIDE,

00:00:01.788 --> 00:00:04.632
your first visit is different from the visits that follow.

00:00:05.002 --> 00:00:08.407
This film is for people starting tremelimumab and durvalumab

00:00:08.407 --> 00:00:11.912
for hepatocellular carcinoma, a cancer that starts in the liver.

00:00:12.202 --> 00:00:15.063
The aim is to control the cancer and help people live longer

00:00:15.063 --> 00:00:17.072
when surgery to remove it is not an option.

00:00:17.423 --> 00:00:21.812
STRIDE stands for Single Tremelimumab, Regular Interval Durvalumab.

00:00:22.102 --> 00:00:25.973
Both medicines help immune cells attack cancer, but they act at different points.

00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:29.367
Tremelimumab, also called Imjudo, blocks

00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:33.490
a brake called CTLA-4 as immune cells become activated.

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:39.710
Durvalumab, or Imfinzi, blocks a signal called PD-L1 that can switch off the attack.

00:00:39.985 --> 00:00:42.235
Tremelimumab is given once at the start.

00:00:42.805 --> 00:00:45.295
Durvalumab continues every four weeks.

00:00:45.685 --> 00:00:48.455
The study behind this treatment is called HIMALAYA.

00:00:48.965 --> 00:00:52.062
It was an international phase three trial in people with liver

00:00:52.062 --> 00:00:55.415
cancer who had not yet received drug treatment for advanced disease.

00:00:55.905 --> 00:00:58.175
Patients were assigned to treatment groups by chance.

00:00:58.505 --> 00:01:02.464
At three years, about thirty-one in every hundred were alive with STRIDE,

00:01:02.464 --> 00:01:06.495
compared with twenty in every hundred with sorafenib, a tablet treatment.

00:01:06.765 --> 00:01:08.155
These are group results.

00:01:08.645 --> 00:01:10.615
Your team can explain what they mean for you.

00:01:11.025 --> 00:01:14.435
On day one, you receive both medicines through a drip into a vein.

00:01:14.765 --> 00:01:17.595
First comes tremelimumab over one hour.

00:01:17.985 --> 00:01:22.735
After that drip finishes, your team waits one to two hours before starting durvalumab.

00:01:23.045 --> 00:01:25.015
The durvalumab drip takes another hour.

00:01:25.365 --> 00:01:27.577
That makes about three to four hours from the

00:01:27.577 --> 00:01:29.915
start of the first drip to the end of the second.

00:01:30.285 --> 00:01:32.850
Allow extra time at the hospital for blood results,

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:35.455
assessment, preparation and any further observation.

00:01:35.705 --> 00:01:38.095
Four weeks later, the next cycle begins.

00:01:38.465 --> 00:01:42.815
This time you receive just durvalumab, through a drip lasting about an hour.

00:01:43.185 --> 00:01:45.650
The plan repeats every four weeks while the treatment

00:01:45.650 --> 00:01:47.615
is helping and side effects are manageable.

00:01:47.985 --> 00:01:49.795
There is no fixed number of cycles.

00:01:50.245 --> 00:01:53.101
A visit may move if you are unwell or need more tests,

00:01:53.101 --> 00:01:55.215
so use the date your day ward gives you.

00:01:55.565 --> 00:01:58.202
Your team checks your blood count, kidneys,

00:01:58.202 --> 00:02:01.215
liver, blood sugar and thyroid before each cycle.

00:02:01.565 --> 00:02:04.135
They also check for hepatitis B and C before you start.

00:02:04.505 --> 00:02:06.662
With liver cancer, it matters how your blood

00:02:06.662 --> 00:02:08.935
results compare with your own starting levels.

00:02:09.545 --> 00:02:13.175
At each visit, mention any new symptom, even if it has settled.

00:02:13.485 --> 00:02:16.415
It helps the team decide whether it is safe to give the next dose.

00:02:16.925 --> 00:02:19.875
A reaction can happen during a drip or shortly afterwards.

00:02:20.465 --> 00:02:23.442
In HIMALAYA, ten of the three hundred and eighty-eight people

00:02:23.442 --> 00:02:26.135
who received the combination had an infusion reaction.

00:02:26.485 --> 00:02:28.155
That is about three in a hundred.

00:02:28.445 --> 00:02:32.298
Tell the nurse immediately if you feel hot, shivery, itchy or dizzy,

00:02:32.298 --> 00:02:35.875
or if your throat feels tight or breathing becomes difficult.

00:02:36.085 --> 00:02:39.535
Staff can pause the infusion, check you and treat the reaction.

00:02:39.723 --> 00:02:43.141
At home, you may notice tiredness, itchy skin,

00:02:43.141 --> 00:02:46.944
a rash, loose stools, or aching muscles and joints.

00:02:47.594 --> 00:02:50.904
These medicines can also make the immune system inflame healthy organs.

00:02:51.354 --> 00:02:53.444
A small change can be the first sign.

00:02:53.834 --> 00:02:56.424
Ring your oncology team promptly about new symptoms.

00:02:56.914 --> 00:03:00.425
If you develop a fever or suddenly feel unwell, ring the twenty-four-hour

00:03:00.425 --> 00:03:03.484
number straight away so the team can also check for infection.

00:03:03.774 --> 00:03:06.504
Liver inflammation can first show up on a blood test.

00:03:07.034 --> 00:03:10.786
Ring urgently if the whites of your eyes or your skin turn yellow,

00:03:10.786 --> 00:03:14.164
your urine becomes dark, or your belly becomes more swollen.

00:03:14.554 --> 00:03:17.664
Your team will check for inflammation as well as other liver problems.

00:03:18.134 --> 00:03:21.384
For the bowel, ring about new diarrhoea, even if mild.

00:03:21.834 --> 00:03:26.164
Tell the team how often you are going and whether there is pain, blood or mucus.

00:03:26.594 --> 00:03:30.144
A new cough or shortness of breath may be a sign of lung inflammation.

00:03:30.694 --> 00:03:33.584
Ring your oncology team urgently, even between visits.

00:03:33.994 --> 00:03:35.324
Tell them about a new rash.

00:03:35.674 --> 00:03:39.844
Blisters, peeling skin or painful mouth sores need urgent assessment.

00:03:40.314 --> 00:03:42.164
The kidneys can also become inflamed.

00:03:42.474 --> 00:03:44.228
Passing much less urine than usual

00:03:44.228 --> 00:03:46.884
or developing new swelling is another reason to ring.

00:03:47.041 --> 00:03:49.562
Immunotherapy can affect glands that make hormones.

00:03:49.852 --> 00:03:53.482
Thyroid changes can cause tiredness or feeling unusually cold.

00:03:53.812 --> 00:03:57.550
Severe weakness, dizziness, vomiting or a persistent headache

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.982
can mean that cortisol, a hormone your body needs, is low.

00:04:01.472 --> 00:04:04.842
Marked thirst and passing urine much more often can signal diabetes.

00:04:05.092 --> 00:04:09.282
Ring urgently about these symptoms, a racing heartbeat or new muscle weakness.

00:04:09.572 --> 00:04:11.482
Your team can arrange the right tests.

00:04:11.912 --> 00:04:15.002
Reporting symptoms early gives your team time to find the cause.

00:04:15.292 --> 00:04:18.742
They may pause the next dose and use steroids to calm inflammation.

00:04:19.072 --> 00:04:20.742
Sometimes treatment needs to stop.

00:04:21.172 --> 00:04:24.722
A hormone problem may need hormone replacement, occasionally for life.

00:04:25.332 --> 00:04:27.819
If you are prescribed steroids, follow the plan

00:04:27.819 --> 00:04:30.402
your team gives you, including how to reduce them.

00:04:30.812 --> 00:04:33.982
Side effects can first appear months after your last infusion.

00:04:34.272 --> 00:04:38.302
Before you leave the day ward, save your hospital’s twenty-four-hour oncology number.

00:04:38.692 --> 00:04:43.462
Carry your immunotherapy alert card and show it if you see a GP or an emergency team.

00:04:43.872 --> 00:04:48.002
If you feel unwell and cannot reach oncology, seek urgent medical assessment.

00:04:48.392 --> 00:04:50.862
For severe breathing difficulty, severe chest pain

00:04:50.862 --> 00:04:53.102
or collapse, call one one two for an ambulance.

00:04:53.352 --> 00:04:56.102
You can ring for advice even after your treatment has finished.
